---
import { getTechnicalReference } from '@lib/seo/copy';

interface Combo {
  from: string;
  to: string;
  sizeKb: number | null;
}

interface Props {
  combo: Combo;
  intro: string;
}

const { combo, intro } = Astro.props;
const fromLabel = combo.from.toUpperCase();
const toLabel = combo.to.toUpperCase();
const techRef = getTechnicalReference(combo);
---

<section class="mx-auto mt-14 max-w-prose" aria-labelledby="tool-intro-heading">
  <h2
    id="tool-intro-heading"
    class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-fg sm:text-2xl"
  >
    {combo.sizeKb
      ? `How ${fromLabel} to ${toLabel} under ${combo.sizeKb}KB works`
      : `How ${fromLabel} to ${toLabel} conversion works`}
  </h2>

  <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-base text-fg/85 leading-relaxed">
    {intro.split('\n\n').map((para) => (
      <p>{para}</p>
    ))}
  </div>

  <!-- Dynamic Technical Format Comparison Table -->
  <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-surface p-5 sm:p-6 shadow-2xs">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
      <span class="inline-block h-2 w-2 rounded-full bg-accent"></span>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-fg">
        Format Comparison: {fromLabel} vs {toLabel}
      </h3>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-muted mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      Core architectural specifications, byte compression behavior, and container capabilities:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-muted">
            <th class="pb-2.5 font-semibold text-fg/70 w-1/4">Property</th>
            <th class="pb-2.5 font-semibold text-fg w-3/8">{fromLabel} (Source Format)</th>
            <th class="pb-2.5 font-semibold text-accent w-3/8">{toLabel} (Target Format)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-border/60 text-fg/85">
          {techRef.comparisonRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 font-medium text-muted">{row.property}</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 text-fg/90">{row.sourceValue}</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 font-medium text-fg">{row.targetValue}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Engine & Privacy Note -->
    <div class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/30 bg-emerald-500/10 p-3.5 sm:p-4 text-xs text-fg/90 flex items-start gap-3">
      <span class="text-emerald-500 text-base shrink-0 leading-none mt-0.5">🔒</span>
      <div>
        <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Engine &amp; Privacy Note:</strong>
        <span class="ml-1 text-muted">{techRef.engineNote}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- In-Browser Execution Pipeline -->
  <div class="mt-10 space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-fg">
      Four-Stage In-Browser Conversion Pipeline
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-fg/85 leading-relaxed">
      Unlike traditional online file converters that transmit your files to cloud servers, PixelKit executes every conversion pass directly inside your browser memory using hardware-accelerated WebAssembly and HTML5 Canvas APIs:
    </p>

    <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3.5 text-sm text-fg/85">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-surface border border-border text-xs font-semibold text-accent">1</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Client-Side Stream Decoding:</strong>
          When you select a {fromLabel} file, your device reads the binary byte stream directly into an ArrayBuffer without network transmission. Decoders parse container headers, color profiles, and raw pixel bitmaps locally.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-surface border border-border text-xs font-semibold text-accent">2</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Canvas Pre-Processing &amp; Framing:</strong>
          If aspect ratio cropping or pixel resizing is requested, PixelKit resamples the image matrix using bicubic interpolation. When converting from transparent formats to formats like JPG, clean solid color backings are blended automatically.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-surface border border-border text-xs font-semibold text-accent">3</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Adaptive Quantization Engine:</strong>
          {combo.sizeKb
            ? `To guarantee your file stays safely under ${combo.sizeKb}KB, PixelKit runs a fast binary search over discrete quantization tables in a background Web Worker, pinpointing the optimal quality level that respects your budget.`
            : `The encoder compiles high-efficiency quantization tables to ensure the output ${toLabel} preserves sharp visual edges, true color tonality, and high signal-to-noise ratio.`}
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-surface border border-border text-xs font-semibold text-accent">4</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">Instant Blob Download &amp; Zero Residue:</strong>
          The compiled {toLabel} file is exposed as an ephemeral blob URL for instantaneous saving. Once downloaded or cleared, all pixel buffers are immediately released by browser garbage collection.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Deep Technical Optimization Guide -->
  <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-surface p-5 sm:p-6">
    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-fg">
      Deep-Dive: Compression &amp; Format Engineering Principles
    </h3>
    <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-fg/80 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        <strong>Color Profile and Gamut Preservation:</strong> Digital images captured on modern smartphones frequently embed wide color gamuts such as Display P3 or Apple Wide Color. When re-encoding to {toLabel}, PixelKit transforms chromatic coordinate spaces to standard sRGB via WebAssembly color correction matrices. This ensures that skin tones, skies, and vibrant logos display identically across every mobile screen, desktop monitor, and physical printer without color clipping or muddy desaturation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>EXIF Metadata and Orientation Normalization:</strong> Raw camera captures often store orientation flags (EXIF rotation tags 1 through 8) rather than physically rotating the pixel grid. If a viewer ignores this tag, the photo displays sideways or upside down. During conversion, PixelKit extracts the embedded EXIF tag, rotates the bitmap matrix physically into canonical upright orientation, and allows you to strip personal GPS coordinates and camera serial numbers for enhanced security and privacy.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Elimination of Re-Compression Artifacts:</strong> Repeatedly opening, editing, and saving compressed image files introduces generational loss, causing noticeable macroblock ringing and mosquito noise around high-contrast edges. PixelKit performs all cropping, scaling, color filling, and quantization in a single non-destructive memory pipeline, eliminating intermediate re-compression artifacts so your final {toLabel} remains as crisp as possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Multi-Threaded Worker Performance:</strong> Encoding complex formats like WebP, AVIF, or large PDF documents requires intensive mathematical operations including discrete cosine transforms, discrete wavelet transforms, and Huffman coding passes. PixelKit delegates these compute-heavy loops to isolated background Web Worker threads. This architectural decoupling ensures the main browser thread remains 100% responsive at a smooth 60 frames per second, preventing UI stutter or frozen browser tabs even when batch converting high-resolution photo archives.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Offline Progressivity &amp; Native Device Reliability:</strong> Once loaded into your web browser, PixelKit caches all core WebAssembly binaries and conversion scripts in a Service Worker cache. This means you can continue converting, cropping, and compressing {fromLabel} into {toLabel} on airplanes, remote travel destinations, or in enterprise facilities with zero active internet connectivity.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
